{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790984372,"nonce":"1n1nOz/qrfcmwJ8Iftvk1XWFpEE=","message":"Landed as `2270483`.\n\n`column_width` is a defaulted argument on the board's row of `view/kinds.go`, default 32, kind int: \"the narrowest a column goes before the board scrolls sideways; when every column fits they share the width\". `tui/board.go` reads it with `call.Int` into `boardPage.colWidth` and `layout()` uses it; the `minColumnWidth = 20` constant is gone.\n\nThe floor is a new `Min` field on `view.Arg`, 10 for this row, checked in `Arg.check`'s Int case, so `{\"column_width\":4}` is refused at parse time (`view board: column_width is 4, and the smallest is 10: ...`) and `git work view board --help` prints the shape as `int, at least 10`. The floor is the table's, not the renderer's, so both surfaces and the Starlark call get it.\n\nA `column_width` left at 32 stays off the call line, the way the built-in `rank` does; one somebody chose shows like any other argument.\n\nTwo notes on the arithmetic in the ask. Six statuses never were 20 cells each at 120 wide: 6x20 + 3x5 gaps = 135, so 120 already scrolled (5 of 6 visible). 140 is the width that fit them at exactly 20 each, so that is the width the new test and the design doc use: at 140 the default now scrolls and shows the `\u003e` marker, and `{\"column_width\":20}` fits all six. `tui/board_test.go`'s helper window went from 140 to 210 (6x32 + 15 = 207) so that every other board test still draws the whole board, and `TestBoardScrollsSideways` now passes `column_width: 20` to keep its original two-visible-columns geometry at width 50.\n\nDocs: `doc/design/terminal-renderer.md` Board (the minimum-width paragraph is the argument, with why it grew, why it is an argument rather than a constant, and the floor); AGENTS.md's view paragraph names `column_width`, 32 cells. `gofmt`, `go vet ./...` and `go test ./...` pass.","files":null}]}